Third of the rust-rows arc. 24 rows, every refusal pinned as a test, and
unlike WI-gihos THESE ROWS FIRE.

WHY THEY FIRE, AND WHY THAT IS NOT LUCK. WI-gihos measured that the rust
catalogue matches ZERO method-kind primitives across 11,691 call edges: rust
receivers arrive unresolved (rust:external:..., null dst_ref) and
gate_named_entry correctly refuses a method-kind hit with no module hint
(INV-linub L3/L4). FUNCTION-kind rows are the ones that match, and std::env is
almost entirely free functions. Measured A/B on the 42-repo cohort:
std::env::temp_dir NEWLY CLASSIFIES 3 EDGES as host_info_read in 2
repositories. Small, and stated as small -- but it is the first row of this arc
to move an edge at all. The std::process METHOD rows are inert today for
exactly WI-gihos's reason, and that is said here rather than found later.
Starvation delta: nothing freed, nothing stranded, despite four new
method-only modules.

EVERY BOUNDARY IS FIXED BY CROSS-LANGUAGE PRECEDENT RATHER THAN CHOSEN.
python.yaml already rules os.putenv / os.unsetenv / os.chdir as env_write,
os.kill as ipc_send ("Signalling another process"), os.wait / waitpid as
ipc_recv ("Reaping a child collects its exit status - data received from
another process"), and tempfile.gettempdir as host_info_read. Added: rust's
first env_write section (set_var, remove_var, set_current_dir); vars_os under
env_read; temp_dir / home_dir under host_info_read; Child.kill as ipc_send and
Child.{wait,try_wait,wait_with_output} as ipc_recv; and the ChildStdin /
ChildStdout / ChildStderr pipe types carrying their trait methods on the
concrete type, per WI-gihos's keying rule. set_current_dir was PROMISED IN
ADVANCE by this file's own note: "chdir has no row here: python rules it
env_write, and rust's std::env::set_current_dir is not yet audited."

std::thread STAYS ABSENT ENTIRELY -- the judgment this item was filed to make,
recorded with the argument that carries it because no cross-language answer
exists. std::thread reads RUST_MIN_STACK through env::var when it spawns. That
does NOT make thread::spawn an env_read boundary for a caller who never names a
variable. The deciding reason is discrimination, not precedent: the read is
INTERNAL -- it sizes the new thread's stack and its value never reaches the
caller -- so if it counted, locale lookups, TZ, RUST_BACKTRACE and a long tail
of stdlib machinery would all qualify, and env_read would stop distinguishing
"this code reads configuration" from "this code runs on a stdlib that does".
A BOUNDARY THAT EVERYTHING SATISFIES CLASSIFIES NOTHING. Supporting: this file
already declared concurrency primitives absent, and the owner's boundary test
("is it changing state outside of itself") puts a thread inside the process, as
WI-pavob applied to locks. PYTHON IS SILENT, NOT SUPPORTIVE -- threading appears
only in its stdlib_modules name list, with no rows and no completeness entry --
and that is stated plainly rather than dressed up as agreement.

A FOURTH PROBE BUG, and like the first three it returned a plausible wrong
answer rather than an error. The receiver regex required a leading `&`, so
`fn wait_with_output(mut self)` and 46 OTHER BY-VALUE RECEIVERS in process.rs
read as ASSOCIATED FUNCTIONS. Kind is precisely what decides matchability, so
that would have shipped wait_with_output as a function-kind row no method call
could ever match (INV-nular). Re-checked against WI-gihos's 70 already-merged
rows: none was affected, because the by-value names there (by_ref, bytes,
chain, take) were all refused as adapters anyway.

REFUSALS, each with its reason and its test: std::process::id and Child::id
(python deliberately holds os.getpid out); Command's builder setters env /
env_clear / env_remove / envs / current_dir / arg / stdin / stdout / stderr
(they mutate a builder in THIS process and spawn is already rowed -- WI-bupor's
OpenOptionsExt rule); ExitStatus accessors (reading an already-collected
status); Stdio constructors; join_paths / split_paths (pure OsString
manipulation); thread::available_parallelism (python holds os.cpu_count out for
the same reason it holds getpid out).

Noted for a later sweep, not chased: python.yaml's tempfile completeness entry
says "gettempdir rowed under env_read" while the row sits under host_info_read
-- a note that predates the INV-tutar split.

Full suite green: 24,140 passed, 100% coverage, ratchet clean.

